   "q": "<style>.katex-display{overflow-x:auto;overflow-y:hidden;padding:2px 0}</style><span style=\"display:block;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:8px\">Repeat Assessment · 1 Feb 2024 · Part I (b) · 7 pts</span><span style=\"display:block;font-size:17px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.5\">With respect to the set $$\\footnotesize \\Omega=\\left\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2 : x\\in[1,2]\\ \\wedge\\ \\frac{1}{x}\\le y\\right\\}$$we may conclude that \\(\\left(\\frac32,3\\right)\\) is an <span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">1</span> point of \\(\\Omega\\) and<br>\\(\\partial\\Omega=fr(\\Omega)=\\) <span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">2</span><br>Since \\(\\Omega\\) is unbounded, then \\(\\Omega\\) is <b>not</b> <span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">3</span></span><details style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:14px;font-weight:500\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--muted)\">Show original exam item</summary><img src=\"img/feb24-b.png\" alt=\"Original exam item b\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;height:auto;margin-top:8px;border-radius:8px;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line)\"></details>",
   "a": "<p><span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">1</span> <b>interior</b> (also accepted: accumulation / adherent)</p><p><span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">2</span> \\(\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb{R}^2 : (x=1\\wedge y\\ge1)\\ \\vee\\ (x=2\\wedge y\\ge\\tfrac12)\\ \\vee\\ (y=\\tfrac1x\\wedge x\\in[1,2])\\}\\)</p><p><span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">3</span> <b>compact</b></p>"
