   "type": "open",
   "qHtml": true,
   "q": "<style>.katex-display{overflow-x:auto;overflow-y:hidden;padding:2px 0}</style><span style=\"display:block;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--muted);margin-bottom:8px\">Repeat Assessment · 1 Feb 2024 · Part I (c) · 4 pts</span><span style=\"display:block;font-size:17px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.5\">Let \\(g:\\mathbb{R}^2\\to\\mathbb{R}\\) be defined by \\(g(x,y)=-y^2+\\cos(x)\\) and \\(f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\,]-\\infty,-1[\\) be the map whose graphical representation is given by: <i>(see the original for the graph: \\(f\\) decreasing, with horizontal asymptote \\(y=-1\\) as \\(x\\to-\\infty\\))</i><br>Then $$\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(0,-\\infty)}\\frac{-3}{(f\\circ g)(x,y)}=\\boxed{1}$$</span><details style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:14px;font-weight:500\"><summary style=\"cursor:pointer;color:var(--muted)\">Show original exam item</summary><img src=\"img/feb24-c.png\" alt=\"Original exam item c\" loading=\"lazy\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;height:auto;margin-top:8px;border-radius:8px;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line)\"></details>",
   "a": "<p><span style=\"display:inline-block;border:1.5px solid currentColor;border-radius:5px;padding:0 6px;margin:0 2px;font-size:.78em;line-height:1.35;font-weight:700;vertical-align:.08em\">1</span> \\(3\\)</p><p><p><i>As \\(y\\to-\\infty\\), \\(g(x,y)\\to-\\infty\\), and \\(f(z)\\to-1\\) as \\(z\\to-\\infty\\). So the limit is \\(\\frac{-3}{-1}=3\\).</i></p></p>"
